Q: Find strings with longest common prefix A: <p>If it's feasible to read the entire input file into memory then you can do this very efficiently.</p>
<p>Start by sorting the words read from the file. Then you can use <code>bisect.bisect_left</code> to give a starting point in the list from where to start considering the values therein. If the common prefix length is zero you can break your loop. In other words, you'll only ever need to check a subset of the list.</p>
<p>Here's what I suggest:</p>
<pre><code>from bisect import bisect_left
from pathlib import Path

PATH = Path(&quot;~/wordlist.txt&quot;).expanduser()

def cpl(a: str, b: str) -&gt; int:
    r = 0
    for a_, b_ in zip(a, b):
        if a_ == b_:
            r += 1
        else:
            break
    return r

def process(prefix: str, words: list[str]) -&gt; list[str]:
    result: list[str] = []
    if words and prefix:
        mcpl = 0
        pos = bisect_left(words, prefix[0])
        for word in words[pos:]:
            if (pl := cpl(word, prefix)) &gt; mcpl:
                mcpl = pl
                result = [word]
            elif pl == mcpl and pl &gt; 0:
                result.append(word)
            else:
                break
    return result

def most_similar(query: str) -&gt; list[str]:
    with PATH.open(encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;) as f:
        return process(query, sorted(map(str.rstrip, f)))

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    print(most_similar(&quot;app&quot;))
</code></pre>
